00:32:44.106 --> 00:32:45.645
so not keyboard accessible.

00:32:45.904 --> 00:32:49.432
It did not… None of these
plugins helped us with this.

00:32:49.559 --> 00:32:50.712
It's still an A tag.

00:32:51.023 --> 00:32:55.056
This expanded widget, meaning it's
not fully keyboard accessible, so

00:32:55.059 --> 00:32:57.770
the tab key does not work on it.

00:32:58.457 --> 00:33:03.774
So even though a few issues were
fixed, it was most likely the same

00:33:03.793 --> 00:33:07.954
issues for every plugin but not
for our manually created issues.

00:33:07.957 --> 00:33:11.203
And for the image true,
we never spoke about this.

00:33:11.233 --> 00:33:15.803
It has not no alt attribute,
which is also bad.

00:33:15.867 --> 00:33:16.563
We know that.

00:33:17.561 --> 00:33:19.179
There was no fix for this either

00:33:23.928 --> 00:33:30.235
And yeah, the click here links, the text,
the read more text is still visible, not

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:36.094
understandable for screen reader users
as well as the labels for our forms.

00:33:36.585 --> 00:33:37.680
They are not connected.

00:33:37.994 --> 00:33:40.031
So these issues were also not fixed.

00:33:40.916 --> 00:33:45.696
So maybe I'm going to go out on a
limb here, but I would say that code

00:33:45.697 --> 00:33:47.530
cannot be fixed with a single click.

00:33:48.131 --> 00:33:51.019
And the reason for this
is technical limitations.

00:33:51.386 --> 00:33:55.025
Yeah, every browser, operating
system, and user setting is different.

00:33:55.278 --> 00:33:59.713
So while certain changes to your DOM
may occur, they are neither permanent

00:33:59.724 --> 00:34:07.798
nor sustainable and meaningful, and
technical depth will increase a lot

00:34:09.963 --> 00:34:17.442
So plugins are maybe some kind of
helpful to make it, the user experience

00:34:17.444 --> 00:34:22.204
a little bit better, but not really
fixing any of our WCAG compliant issues.

00:34:23.519 --> 00:34:23.708
Yeah.

00:34:24.238 --> 00:34:28.025
The human element, empathy and
skills are still irreplaceable.

00:34:28.127 --> 00:34:32.247
Sounds like a title of a thrilling
movie, but it's simply a very obvious

00:34:32.255 --> 00:34:34.518
fact, in my opinion, of course.

00:34:34.714 --> 00:34:37.581
Technical expertise cannot
be completely outsourced.

00:34:37.586 --> 00:34:42.067
Sure, there are automated tools, but
they only scratch the surface, as we saw.

00:34:42.470 --> 00:34:47.112
To make it truly accessible, a
website, you still need human empathy

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:50.193
and critical thinking and practical
testing that takes into account

00:34:50.194 --> 00:34:51.948
a wide range of user experience.

00:34:52.326 --> 00:34:55.483
Yeah, this is still required,
and manually testing, of course.

00:34:55.488 --> 00:34:58.216
So the structure of a site
and the layout this is

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:03.532
These are things an AI cannot grab- grasp.

00:35:03.691 --> 00:35:08.627
And also writing clean, sustainable
code, using correct ARIA attributes

00:35:08.628 --> 00:35:13.418
and, yeah, ensuring keyboard navigation,
that this actually works seamlessly

00:35:13.446 --> 00:35:16.668
is something only a human can test.

00:35:17.612 --> 00:35:20.606
And also additionally, according
to the latest Web One Million

00:35:20.606 --> 00:35:24.587
report, the use of images on
websiteing has increased quite a bit.

00:35:24.798 --> 00:35:29.875
So we know that images need an
alternative text, and this is also

00:35:29.878 --> 00:35:37.351
something AI cannot do now, to create
an meaningful alternative text.

00:35:37.980 --> 00:35:41.103
So we still need people to write
meaningful image descriptions.

00:35:42.821 --> 00:35:46.080
But what can you do if you
don't know how to code at all?

00:35:46.426 --> 00:35:51.163
I understand as a content creator with no
coding experience it's difficult to create

00:35:51.165 --> 00:35:55.578
an accessible website because you have
to rely 100% on the tool you're using.

00:35:56.369 --> 00:36:00.989
But you cannot only hope that
themes are getting better over time.

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:05.863
And also without forced
to pay for code quality.

00:36:06.471 --> 00:36:09.517
So I would say the simpler the
website, the more likely you're

00:36:09.533 --> 00:36:11.523
able to make it WCAG compliant.

00:36:11.947 --> 00:36:15.285
Yeah, the more complicated websites
get, the sooner you should invest

00:36:15.285 --> 00:36:21.257
in an accessibility developer to fix
common issues Rather to rely on some

00:36:21.259 --> 00:36:26.452
plugin to do the job, or that a theme
will become more accessible over time.

00:36:27.342 --> 00:36:30.904
How can you do… How can
you make the most of it?

00:36:31.384 --> 00:36:33.438
There's still a few things you can do.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.311
Choose a theme that is labeled
as accessibility ready.

00:36:36.569 --> 00:36:39.525
Yeah, stick to a correct
heading and landmark structure.

00:36:39.528 --> 00:36:41.571
Make sure the color
contrast is high enough.

00:36:41.824 --> 00:36:44.378
Include a good alternative
text for your images.

00:36:45.167 --> 00:36:50.039
Yeah, make sure that your buttons
and links are descriptive.

00:36:50.745 --> 00:36:56.598
Yeah, maybe you can do a little search
or stick overflow and add a small line

00:36:56.644 --> 00:37:01.283
of CSS code for example, for outlines
or hover effect, that this also meets

00:37:01.283 --> 00:37:03.087
the color contrast requirements.

00:37:03.413 --> 00:37:07.323
This could be things you may do
with a little bit help of code.

00:37:07.512 --> 00:37:09.060
Of course, enable skip links, yeah.

00:37:10.188 --> 00:37:13.469
So we can only do our best with
the tools we have available.

00:37:13.498 --> 00:37:16.471
I would end my presentation.

00:37:16.686 --> 00:37:17.121
Thank you.

